3.4 Speech System Commands
==========================

This section documents the main Emacsvox commands for controlling the
text-to-speech (TTS) system.  The exact key bindings generated from the
current keymaps appear in the *note (emacsvox-reference)Emacsvox
Keymaps::.

* Menu:

* Controlling Echo::             Character, Word and Line Echo.
* Speech Output  Control::       Indicating case, capitalization and indentation.
* Miscellaneous:: Miscellaneous TTS Commands.


File: emacsvox.info,  Node: Controlling Echo,  Next: Speech Output Control,  Up: Speech System

3.4.1 Character, Word And Line Echo.
------------------------------------

By default, Emacsvox speaks characters as they are typed -- this is
called character echo; Words are spoken as they are completed -- this is
called word echo.  Emacsvox can also optionally speak each line as it is
typed -- this is called line echo.

   Character, word and line echo can be toggled -- either in the current
buffer -- or for all buffers (globally).  To toggle the specific echo
functionality for all buffers, precede the specific command with ‘C-u’.
Note that in the documentation below, this use of ‘C-u’ is indicated
using the common Emacs terminology of *prefix arg* or *interactive
prefix arg*.

‘C-e d k’
     ‘emacsvox-toggle-character-echo’

     Toggle state of Emacsvox character echo.  Interactive PREFIX arg
     means toggle the global default value, and then set the current
     local value to the result.

‘C-e d w’
     ‘emacsvox-toggle-word-echo’

     Toggle state of Emacsvox word echo.  Interactive PREFIX arg means
     toggle the global default value, and then set the current local
     value to the result.

‘C-e d l’
     ‘emacsvox-toggle-line-echo’

     Toggle state of Emacsvox line echo.  Interactive PREFIX arg means
     toggle the global default value, and then set the current local
     value to the result.


File: emacsvox.info,  Node: Speech Output Control,  Next: Miscellaneous,  Prev: Controlling Echo,  Up: Speech System

3.4.2 Setting TTS Characteristics.
----------------------------------

Emacsvox user commands can set different characteristics of the speech
output such as speech rate and punctuations mode.

   Emacsvox provides settings that affect how text attributes such as
capitalization and indentation are conveyed.  Each feature has a master
switch and a separately selected presentation, so enabling the feature
does not force one fixed sound.  These settings can be made locally in a
given buffer or be applied to all buffers by preceding these commands
with ‘C-u’.

‘C-e d r’
     ‘tts-set-rate’

     Set speaking RATE for the TTS.  Interactive PREFIX arg means set
     the global default value, and then set the current local value to
     the result.

‘C-e d f’
     ‘tts-set-character-scale’

     Set scale FACTOR for speech rate.  Speech rate is scaled by this
     factor when speaking characters.  Interactive PREFIX arg means set
     the global default value, and then set the current local value to
     the result.

‘C-e d DIGIT’
     ‘tts-set-predefined-rate’

     Set speech rate to one of nine predefined levels using digit keys
     ‘0’ through ‘9’.  Interactive PREFIX arg says to set the rate
     globally.

‘C-e d p’
     ‘tts-set-punctuations’

     Set punctuation mode to MODE. Possible values are ‘some’, ‘all’, or
     ‘none’.  Interactive PREFIX arg means set the global default value,
     and then set the current local value to the result.

‘C-e d s’
     ‘tts-toggle-split-caps’

     Toggle split caps mode.  Split caps mode is useful when reading
     Hungarian notation in program source code.  Interactive PREFIX arg
     means toggle the global default value, and then set the current
     local value to the result.

‘C-e d c’
     ‘tts-toggle-caps’

     Toggle capitalization indication.  Interactive PREFIX arg means
     toggle the global default value, then set the current local value
     to the result.

‘C-e d C’
     ‘emacsvox-set-capitalization-presentation’

     Choose ‘spoken’, ‘tone’, ‘spoken-tone’, ‘custom’, or ‘none’, and
     preview representative text.  ‘custom’ publishes facts for personal
     Aural Presentation rules without adding built-in output.  A current
     Omnivox server applies the selected capitalization presentation to
     isolated letters as well as queued words; older speech servers
     retain their engine-specific isolated-letter behavior.

   In addition, Emacsvox can convey the indentation of lines as they are
spoken -- This is relevant when programming and is the default when
working with program source.

‘C-e d i’
     ‘emacsvox-toggle-audio-indentation’

     Toggle state of Emacsvox audio indentation.  Interactive PREFIX arg
     means toggle the global default value, and then set the current
     local value to the result.

‘C-e d I’
     ‘emacsvox-set-indentation-presentation’

     Choose spoken column, duration-coded tone, rising-pitch tone,
     either tone combined with speech, ‘custom’, or ‘none’.  The command
     previews a four-column indentation sample.  Indentation tones
     complete before the line is spoken so they do not mask its first
     phoneme.


File: emacsvox.info,  Node: Miscellaneous,  Prev: Speech Output Control,  Up: Speech System

3.4.3 Miscellaneous Speech Commands
-----------------------------------

Speech can be stopped explicitly using command ‘tts-stop’.  In normal
navigation, replaceable speech supersedes older work in the same
replacement domain while ordered and urgent output is preserved.  The
speech server can be stopped and restarted for cases where the user
wants to switch to a different server -- or in the rare case to nuke a
runaway speech server.

‘C-e s’
     ‘tts-stop’

     Stop speech now.

‘C-e d q’
     ‘tts-toggle-quiet’

     Toggle state of the speech device between being quiet and
     talkative.  Useful if you want to continue using an Emacs session
     that has emacsvox loaded but wish to make the speech shut up.
     Optional argument PREFIX specifies whether speech is turned off in
     the current buffer or in all buffers.

‘C-e C-s’
     ‘tts-restart’

     Use this to stop the currently running speech server and restart
     it.  Useful if you want to switch to another synthesizer while
     emacsvox is running.  Also useful for emergency stopping of speech.

   Finally, here are the remaining commands available via the TTS
related keymap ‘C-e d’.

‘C-e d a’
     ‘tts-add-cleanup-pattern’

     Add this pattern to the list of repeating patterns that are cleaned
     up.  Optional interactive prefix arg deletes this pattern if
     previously added.  Cleaning up repeated patterns results in
     emacsvox speaking the pattern followed by a repeat count instead of
     speaking all the characters making up the pattern.  Thus, by adding
     the repeating pattern '.'  (this is already added by default)
     emacsvox will say "aw fifteen dot" when speaking the string
     "..............."  instead of "period period period period".

‘C-e d d’
     ‘tts-select-server’

     Select a speech server interactively.  This will be the server that
     is used when you next call either M-x tts-initialize or C-e C-s.
     Argument PROGRAM specifies the speech server program.

‘C-e d <SPACE>’
     ‘tts-toggle-splitting-on-white-space’

     Toggle splitting of speech on white space.  This affects the
     internal state of emacsvox that decides if we split text purely by
     clause boundaries, or also include whitespace.  By default,
     emacsvox sends a clause at a time to the speech device.  This
     produces fluent speech for normal use.  However in modes such as
     'shell-mode' and some programming language modes, clause markers
     appear infrequently, and this can result in large amounts of text
     being sent to the speech device at once, making the system
     unresponsive when asked to stop talking.  Splitting on white space
     makes emacsvox's stop command responsive.  However, when splitting
     on white space, the speech sounds choppy since the synthesizer is
     getting a word at a time.

‘C-e d <RETURN>’
     ‘tts-set-chunk-separator-syntax’

     Interactively set how text is split in chunks.  See the Emacs
     documentation on syntax tables for details on how characters are
     classified into various syntactic classes.  Argument S specifies
     the syntax class.

‘C-e d V’
     ‘tts-speak-version’

     Use this to find out which version of the TTS firmware you are
     running.

‘C-e d z’
     ‘emacsvox-zap-tts’

     Send this command to the TTS engine directly.


File: emacsvox.info,  Node: Voice-lock,  Next: Status Information,  Prev: Speech System,  Up: Basic Usage

3.5 Voice Lock Mode
===================

Toggle voice lock globally with ‘M-x’ ‘global-voice-lock-mode’.  Its
current default binding is in *note (emacsvox-reference)Emacsvox
Keymaps::.

   Voice-lock is turned on automatically in all buffers that support it
when Emacsvox is started.

   Emacsvox can be set to disable voice lock in all of the major modes
that support it.  To do so, insert the following statement into your
Emacs initialization file:

     (global-voice-lock-mode -1)

   The characteristics of the different voice personalities used by
voice lock mode vary according to the capabilities of the speech
synthesizer.  The definitions applicable to the Dectalk family of
synthesizers are contained in ‘dectalk-voices.el’, which is supplied as
part of the Emacsvox distribution.  The Emacsvox distribution also
contains voice definitions for many other synthesizers.

   Voice-lock maps faces and other mode-provided text properties to
speech personalities.  Integrations such as EWW use those properties and
document structure to provide aural distinctions; the active mode and
voice settings, not an inherited Web style sheet, determine current
behavior.  For historical context on Aural CSS, see *note
(emacsvox-heritage)Audio Formatting And Aural CSS::.


