12 Term Terminal Access
***********************

Emacsvox speech-enables the built-in Emacs Term terminal emulator
through the module named ‘emacsvox-eterm’.  The module name is
inherited; users do not install a separate Eterm package.

   For a new full-terminal workflow, prefer *note EAT Terminal Access::.
EAT has the richer current guide for bounded screen output,
terminal-side completion, scrollback, alternate screens, and
asynchronous rendering.  Use built-in Term when avoiding an additional
package matters or an application specifically works better there.  For
ordinary line-oriented shell commands, ‘M-x shell’ is usually simpler
than either terminal emulator.

* Menu:

* Starting Term::
* Term Input Modes::
* Term Speech Feedback::
* Reviewing The Term Screen::
* Term Help And Recovery::


File: emacsvox.info,  Node: Starting Term,  Next: Term Input Modes,  Up: Running Terminal Based Applications

12.1 Starting Term
==================

Run ‘M-x term <RET>’.  At the ‘Run program:’ prompt, accept the proposed
shell or enter another executable and press <RET>.  Emacsvox currently
makes the selected Term window fill the frame when ‘term’ or ‘ansi-term’
starts.  This changes only the window layout: it does not kill the
buffers that were displayed in the other windows.

   Term owns terminal emulation and the child program owns its prompts,
input, and completion.  Emacsvox observes the rendered Term buffer and
adds speech feedback and review commands.  See *note (emacs)Term Mode::
for the emulator itself and *note (emacsvox-reference)emacsvox-eterm::
for the complete Emacsvox API inventory.


